---
// Case: Tealucent, design system for goconut's workplace management software, designed for user generated design
// (agreed wording, 20 Oct 2026). Facts from befund/cases/tealucent.md: goconut sales deck of June 2022 ("Transparenz &
// Überblick"), Sketch file of Dec 2022 (mobile app, formerly Kapago), Robert's boards (palette, token architecture,
// components). Robert, 27 Oct 2026: lead UX at goconut for the web app, design system by him alone, base UX of the
// mobile app by ugd, still in use at goconut; the design was then used for the mobile app too, and the four modes are
// light, dark, translucent and action (Robert, 27 Oct 2026). From the web app on, when goconut became a startup of its
// own, UX and design system by Robert alone; an in-house designer worked mainly on marketing (Robert, 27 Oct 2026). Left out: the ten test participants, the share of desktop and mobile use. Only boards without photos of people.
import { row } from '../../lib/row';
import CaseLayout from '../../components/case/CaseLayout.astro';
import Shot from '../../components/case/Shot.astro';
import ScrubFigure from '../../components/stage/ScrubFigure.astro';
import name from '../../assets/cases/tealucent/name.jpg';
import palette from '../../assets/cases/tealucent/palette.jpg';
import dates from '../../assets/cases/tealucent/board-04-2.jpg';
import tokens from '../../assets/cases/tealucent/tokens.jpg';
import components from '../../assets/cases/tealucent/components.jpg';
import bookings from '../../assets/cases/tealucent/board-03.jpg';

const half = '(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 45vw';
---

<CaseLayout
  title="Tealucent"
  years="2022–24"
  summary="Workplace software that promises transparency, given a look that is literally transparent: one colour, translucent layers."
  meta={[
    ['Role', 'Lead UX for the web app; design system, by me alone'],
    ['Client', 'goconut workplace management, Konstanz; base UX of the mobile app by user generated design'],
    ['Period', '2022 – 2024'],
    ['Team', 'UX and design system by me alone; an in-house designer worked mainly on marketing'],
    ['Status', 'In use at goconut today'],
  ]}
  links={[{ href: 'https://goconut.com', label: 'goconut.com' }]}
  film="tealucent"
  stage="tealucent"
  next={{ href: '/work/conactive/', title: 'Conactive' }}
>
  <section>
    <h2>The problem</h2>
    <p class="c-lead">A booking app had to become the place where a company sees how its office is really used.</p>
    <p>
      goconut lets people book a desk in the office, at home or in a coworking space, book rooms, set their status and
      track their time. To companies it promises transparency and overview: who is where, how much work happens remotely,
      how the space is used. In 2022 it was still a mobile app. It had to grow into a web hub that a small developer team
      could build and keep consistent.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What I did</h2>
    <p class="c-lead">A visual language and a token structure the developers could implement directly.</p>
    <p>
      The base UX of the mobile app came from user generated design. When goconut turned the app into a web app and
      became a startup of its own, I was responsible for its UX and design system on my own. An in-house designer
      worked mainly on marketing in that time. I named it Tealucent, after teal and translucent: the
      colour system, the components, the modes and the tokens that connect the design to the code. The mobile app then took it over too.
    </p>
    <figure class="c-wide">
      <Shot
        src={name}
        source="Tealucent design system"
        label="Name"
        alt="Two overlapping circles: teal, a solid gradient, and translucent, almost invisible; where they overlap, tealucent design"
        widths={[800, 1600]}
        sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 75vw"
      />
      <figcaption>Teal and translucent: the brand colour and the idea of seeing through.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Decisions</h2>
    <p class="c-lead">One colour in ten steps, layers you can see through, tokens in three tiers.</p>
    <p>
      <strong>One colour, not a palette.</strong> goconut had two brand colours, teal and orange. I built the whole
      interface from the teal in ten steps, from 100 % to white, and kept orange for the one thing that has to stand out.
      Fewer colours meant fewer decisions for the developers and a calmer screen for the people using it.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Transparency you can see.</strong> Panels are translucent and lie over the floor plan and the calendar, so
      what is happening in the office stays visible underneath. The promise of the product became the principle of the
      design.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Tokens in three tiers.</strong> A value becomes a global token, an alias token switches between light, dark,
      translucent and action modes, and component tokens use the aliases. A new mode changes the aliases, not every
      component.
    </p>
    <figure class="c-wide">
      <ScrubFigure kind="tealucent-tokens" ratio="1200 / 520" narrow="600 / 910" />
      <figcaption>One alias, four modes: the dropdown and the input take their colour from action_background_ in each mode, and their own tokens stay the same.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="c-split">
      <figure>
        <Shot
          src={dates}
          source="Tealucent design system"
          label="Date bar, dark"
          alt="A date bar from Monday 10 to Sunday 16 on dark teal, each state labelled with its colour token, from gg_75 to gg_00"
          widths={[800, 1600]}
          sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 60vw"
        />
        <figcaption>Every state of a date bar is a step of the same teal.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <Shot
          src={palette}
          source="Tealucent design system"
          label="Colours"
          alt="The palette: brand orange and brand teal, and the app colours from 100 % teal through 85, 75, 50, 40, 20, 15, 10 and 5 % to white, each with its token and hex value"
          widths={[600, 1200]}
          sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 30vw"
        />
        <figcaption>Two brand colours, ten app colours from one teal.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
    <figure class="c-wide">
      <Shot
        src={tokens}
        source="Tealucent design system"
        label="Tokens and modes"
        alt="Token architecture: values become global tokens such as teal_20 and teal_85, an alias token action_background with the modes light, dark, side and trans, and component tokens for dropdown and input; below, the four modes darkmode, lightmode, translucent and action module in one layout"
        widths={[800, 1600]}
        sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 75vw"
      />
      <figcaption>Value, global token, alias with four modes, component token.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="c-pair" style={row(components, bookings)}>
      <figure>
        <Shot
          src={components}
          source="Tealucent design system"
          label="Components"
          alt="Component board: tabs, date bar, calendar with week numbers, dropdown, time picker, a list of people with placeholder names and a set of icons"
          widths={[800, 1600]}
          sizes={half}
        />
        <figcaption>Components, from tabs and calendar to the people list.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <Shot
          src={bookings}
          source="Tealucent design system"
          label="Booking types"
          alt="Booking types as gradient bars: remote work, equipment, creative and holiday, with times, orange for the creative booking"
          widths={[800, 1600]}
          sizes={half}
        />
        <figcaption>Booking types: remote work, equipment, creative, holiday.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Result</h2>
    <p class="c-lead">A system small enough to keep consistent, and still in use.</p>
    <p>
      Tealucent gave goconut one visual language across mobile and web: ten shades of one colour, an orange signal, four
      modes, IBM Plex Sans and Mono, and tokens that tie the design to the code. goconut still uses it today.
    </p>
    <dl class="c-numbers">
      <div><dt>1</dt><dd>colour, in ten steps from teal to white</dd></div>
      <div><dt>4</dt><dd>modes: light, dark, translucent, action</dd></div>
      <div><dt>3</dt><dd>tiers of tokens: global, alias, component</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Timeline</h2>
    <ol class="c-timeline">
      <li><time>2022</time><span>goconut is a mobile app; the brief is a web hub for companies.</span></li>
      <li><time>2023</time><span>Tealucent: one colour, translucent layers, tokens.</span></li>
      <li><time>2024</time><span>Further work as lead UX for the web app.</span></li>
      <li><time>Today</time><span>goconut still runs on Tealucent.</span></li>
    </ol>
  </section>
</CaseLayout>
